;; demos/scenarios/ed-tuesday/config.edn -- ADR-0104, the "A" half of
;; the author's 2026-08-10 "c." ruling (ADR-0103): a scripted single ED
;; shift, weighted toward quick emergent presentations, contrasted
;; against clinic-decade's own population-scale, ambulatory-heavy mix
;; (demos/scenarios/clinic-decade/, ten-year horizon, sparse traffic).
;;
;; The :pathways pool below is genuinely weighted/RNG-drawn -- five
;; scripted ED trajectories, weighted toward the fast/common end (see
;; the per-entry comments; roughly the real-world admission fraction,
;; ~15%, tuned by live-probe below). The ambulatory module tail is
;; authored as a DISJOINT cohort instead of a second weighted pool:
;; ADR-0104's own verification step (`ehrt.sim.run/
;; incompatible-assignments`, `ehrt.sim-engine.engine/assign-pathway`/
;; `assign-module`) found that a SECOND weighted `:module-assignment`
;; pool would apply to every ordinal the ED pool doesn't explicitly
;; cover -- an RNG-coincident double-assignment (an admission-bearing ED
;; pathway AND a module drawn for the same patient) that the static
;; incompatible-assignment check cannot see coming, since it only
;; reports CERTAIN conflicts. `ModuleAssignment`'s own pool member has
;; no "no module" option (ehrt.patient-simulator.gmf/ModuleAssignment), so
;; a weighted module pool always resolves every uncovered ordinal to
;; SOME module -- there is no way to make it apply to only a minority of
;; patients. The sanctioned shape instead lists the module-tail ordinals
;; explicitly, in BOTH :pathways (an empty override -- the documented
;; module-only-patient pattern, engine.clj's own `run` docstring) and
;; :module-assignment (an explicit {:patient-ordinal :module-id}): every
;; ordinal not named here falls through to the ED pool alone, and every
;; named ordinal never draws from it -- conflict-free by construction,
;; not merely undetected by the static check.
;;
;; :facility bumps ONLY the Emergency ward's surge capacity (6 -> 16)
;; versus sim-model/default-facility: with 0 regular ED beds, every ED
;; admission boards on surge immediately (the ladder's own "surge only
;; when full" rule is trivially satisfied at 0 beds), so surge IS the
;; ED's real capacity -- a believable "busy shift" needs more of it than
;; the small-on-purpose default ships. Renal/Cardiology stay at the
;; default 4 beds + 2 surge; this file's own README has the live-probed
;; numbers this pacing (:arrival-gap, the dwell ranges below) was tuned
;; against to hold without capacity exhaustion.
;;
;; :module-horizon-days 90 (~3 months) is short relative to
;; clinic-decade's 3650 (10 years), by this session's own instruction,
;; but a disclosed finding rides along: at THIS thin a tail (8
;; patients), the chosen quick modules (sore_throat/sinusitis/
;; bronchitis/ear_infections) produced ZERO live encounters at every
;; horizon actually tried (14, 90, and even 3650 days -- live-probed,
;; not asserted) with this seed. The mechanism, read from sore_throat's
;; own source (`components/sim/resources/sim/modules/sore_throat.json`,
;; the `Potential_Infection` state): onset is a monthly-Delay-gated
;; complex_transition at roughly 0.5-1% probability per month, so most
;; of a patient's simulated history -- and most of any short forward
;; window -- produces nothing at all; clinic-decade's own README
;; discloses the same low-incidence shape at population scale (68
;; messages from 200 patients over 10 years). This tail is authored
;; anyway, at low weight, per the driving prompt's own ask for one --
;; see this scenario's own README for the actual witnessed number, not
;; silently retuned to force a nonzero result.
{:facility {:id :general-hospital
            ;; ARC 3B SWEEP 2 (ADR-0174 ruling D1, 2026-08-27):
            ;; `:turnaround-minutes` is the range EACH LEG of the bed
            ;; cycle draws from (dirty -> cleaning, cleaning -> ready),
            ;; so a ward's whole turnaround is twice it. These are the
            ;; shipped class defaults written out explicitly; the cycle
            ;; itself is off unless `:bed-cycle` is set below.
            :wards [{:id :ed :name "Emergency" :beds 0 :surge-slots 16
                     :surge-format "%s-H%02d" :class :ed :turnaround-minutes [5 15]}
                    {:id :renal :name "Renal" :beds 4 :surge-slots 2
                     :surge-format "%s-H%02d" :class :inpatient :turnaround-minutes [15 30]}
                    {:id :cardiology :name "Cardiology" :beds 4 :surge-slots 2
                     :surge-format "%s-H%02d" :class :inpatient :turnaround-minutes [15 30]}]}
 :arrival-gap 30
 :pathways [;; Walk-in, quick turnaround -- the ED's own bread and butter.
            {:pathway {:name "ed-fast-track"
                       :steps [{:type :admission :location "Emergency" :reason "Minor laceration"}
                               {:type :delay :from 30 :to 90}
                               {:type :discharge}]}
             :weight 55}
            ;; Worked up, one lab panel, then home.
            {:pathway {:name "ed-observe-and-discharge"
                       :steps [{:type :admission :location "Emergency" :reason "Chest pain, low risk"}
                               {:type :delay :from 60 :to 120}
                               {:type :order :profile :cbc}
                               {:type :delay :from 60 :to 150}
                               {:type :discharge}]}
             :weight 30}
            ;; Admitted, short inpatient dwell.
            {:pathway {:name "ed-admit-renal"
                       :steps [{:type :admission :location "Emergency" :reason "Acute kidney injury"}
                               {:type :delay :from 45 :to 120}
                               {:type :transfer :location "Renal"}
                               {:type :delay :from 240 :to 960}
                               {:type :discharge}]}
             :weight 8}
            {:pathway {:name "ed-admit-cardiology"
                       :steps [{:type :admission :location "Emergency" :reason "Chest pain, high risk"}
                               {:type :delay :from 45 :to 120}
                               {:type :transfer :location "Cardiology"}
                               {:type :delay :from 240 :to 960}
                               {:type :discharge}]}
             :weight 5}
            ;; The rare, resource-heavy trauma case -- imaging in the ED,
            ;; then admitted for the longest dwell in the pool.
            {:pathway {:name "ed-trauma-imaging-admit"
                       :steps [{:type :admission :location "Emergency" :reason "Multi-system trauma"}
                               {:type :delay :from 30 :to 90}
                               {:type :order :profile :cbc}
                               {:type :delay :from 45 :to 90}
                               {:type :transfer :location "Renal"}
                               {:type :delay :from 480 :to 1440}
                               {:type :discharge}]}
             :weight 2}
            ;; The ambulatory module tail -- disjoint from the ED pool
            ;; above by explicit ordinal, per this file's own header
            ;; comment. Empty pathway: the module's own compiled content
            ;; is the whole of this patient's queue (engine.clj's own
            ;; module-only-patient pattern).
            {:patient-ordinal 6 :pathway {:name "module-only" :steps []}}
            {:patient-ordinal 18 :pathway {:name "module-only" :steps []}}
            {:patient-ordinal 30 :pathway {:name "module-only" :steps []}}
            {:patient-ordinal 42 :pathway {:name "module-only" :steps []}}
            {:patient-ordinal 54 :pathway {:name "module-only" :steps []}}
            {:patient-ordinal 66 :pathway {:name "module-only" :steps []}}
            {:patient-ordinal 78 :pathway {:name "module-only" :steps []}}
            {:patient-ordinal 90 :pathway {:name "module-only" :steps []}}]
 :modules ["sore_throat" "sinusitis" "bronchitis" "ear_infections"]
 :module-assignment [{:patient-ordinal 6 :module-id "sore_throat"}
                      {:patient-ordinal 18 :module-id "sinusitis"}
                      {:patient-ordinal 30 :module-id "bronchitis"}
                      {:patient-ordinal 42 :module-id "ear_infections"}
                      {:patient-ordinal 54 :module-id "sore_throat"}
                      {:patient-ordinal 66 :module-id "sinusitis"}
                      {:patient-ordinal 78 :module-id "bronchitis"}
                      {:patient-ordinal 90 :module-id "ear_infections"}]
 ;; ARC 3A PART 4 (ADR-0173 ruling D1's commit 2, 2026-08-26): the
 ;; demographic fold, TURNED ON. This is the opt-in -- `:persons` absent
 ;; is the byte-identical path, and this file is one of the six corpora
 ;; that stop taking it.
 ;;
 ;; `:count` IS TWICE THE ARRIVAL COUNT, and both the shape and the
 ;; factor are measurements rather than preferences. With `:persons`
 ;; present an arrival BINDS to a person, and a repeat arrival of
 ;; somebody already registered queues NOTHING (ADR-0173's own first
 ;; tabled deviation: a second `:admission` for a discharged patient
 ;; violates `admission-only-when-new`). So a small pool silently
 ;; deletes clinical content -- probed at `:count` 50 against 100
 ;; arrivals, only 50 patients registered and three quarters of this
 ;; scenario's own module walks never happened.
 ;;
 ;; One person per arrival is not enough either, and the generator-side
 ;; COVERAGE GATE is what said so: at `:count` 100 the birthday paradox
 ;; still makes better than a third of the arrivals repeats, and the
 ;; module coverage that thinned took this scenario's only
 ;; module-compiled `:admission`/`:discharge` pair with it --
 ;; `gated-runs-collectively-produce-every-emittable-event-type` went
 ;; red on exactly those two kinds. At twice the arrival count the
 ;; collision rate drops enough that both come back.
 ;;
 ;; `:years` IS TWENTY, not the ten `ehrt.sim.run/default-person-years`
 ;; would give, and that is a measurement too: at 10 years this
 ;; population produced ZERO occupational injuries (the hazard is
 ;; conditioned on `:employed` person-years, and most of a ten-year walk
 ;; is not), so the clinical hook would have been declared and never
 ;; witnessed. At 20 it fires. A run's own log therefore extends to the
 ;; PERSON horizon, well past its clinical content -- that is what a
 ;; demographic timeline is, and this scenario's own README carries the
 ;; re-witnessed figures.
 :persons {:count 200 :years 20}

 ;; ARC 3B SWEEP 1 (ADR-0174 rulings A1/E1, 2026-08-26): the ENCOUNTER
 ;; HORIZON, LIFTED. This is the opt-in -- `:encounters` absent is the
 ;; byte-identical path, and this file is one of the six corpora that
 ;; stop taking it.
 ;;
 ;; IT CHANGES WHAT THE PARAGRAPH ABOVE MEANS, and that is said here
 ;; rather than left for a reader to notice. A repeat arrival no longer
 ;; queues NOTHING: with no encounter open it opens a SECOND one, on the
 ;; same patient and the same MRN, which is what an MPI under test needs
 ;; to see. So the pool-sizing argument above is no longer about
 ;; DELETED clinical content -- the content a collision used to cost is
 ;; now a return visit -- but the sizing itself is kept unchanged, for
 ;; two reasons: the coverage gate it was tuned against still reads the
 ;; same population, and re-tuning a pool in the same commit that lifts
 ;; the horizon would leave any corpus delta with two possible causes.
 ;;
 ;; WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE: a repeat arrival landing while the patient's
 ;; first encounter is still OPEN still opens nothing (`decide
 ;; :repeat-arrival`), and one landing on a `:merged` or `:expired`
 ;; patient still opens nothing either. Both terminals stay absorbing.
 :encounters true
 :module-horizon-days 90}
